Paxman was still alive."}, {"n": 2, "title": "THE SECRET ROOM", "body": "The next morning, Sam walked into the kitchen.

He still couldn't stop thinking about what had happened the night before.

The bacon.

The strange noise.

And most of all, the cabinet door.

Sam tried to forget about it as he picked up a stack of dishes.

He began putting them away one by one.

Plate.

Bowl.

Cup.

Then he reached for the last dish.

Sam opened the cabinet.

He froze.

Something was moving inside.

A flap was flapping around wildly, as if something behind it was trying to get through.

Sam stared at it.

\"What is that?\"

The flap suddenly stopped.

Sam blinked.

\"I must be dreaming.\"

He rubbed his eyes and looked again.

The flap was still there.

Slowly, Sam reached forward and grabbed it.

He pulled it open.

And then he saw him.

Sam's eyes widened.

Standing inside the hidden room was...

Paxman.

He was sitting at a desk covered with papers, books, and strange equipment. He was completely focused on his research.

Sam couldn't believe what he was seeing.

Paxman was alive.

\"PAXMAN?!\"

Sam screamed.

Paxman looked up.

Sam stumbled backward in shock.

\"No way!\"

Paxman stared at his brother.

\"Sam?\"

Sam couldn't move.

His mind was racing.

He had seen Paxman die.

So how was he standing right there?

Then Sam noticed what was written across the papers on the desk.

CLONING FOOD.

Sam stared at the words.

\"What are you doing?\"

Paxman quickly turned toward his research.

\"Sam, I can explain.\"

Sam's mouth dropped open.

Before he could say another word—

Sam woke up.

He sat straight up in bed.

He looked around.

His room was quiet.

Nash was sleeping nearby.

Sam breathed heavily.

He looked at the clock.

It was morning.

Sam put his hand over his face.

\"It was just a dream...\"

He slowly got out of bed.

But little did Sam know...

That dream was about to become reality."}, {"n": 3, "title": "THE VISION", "body": "Sam couldn't stop thinking about his dream.

It felt too real.

He could still remember opening the strange flap inside the cabinet.

He could still remember seeing Paxman.

And he could still remember the words on the papers.

CLONING FOOD.

Sam finally decided he had enough.

He got out of bed and walked down the hallway to his dad's room.

\"Dad?\"

His dad looked up.

\"What's wrong?\"

\"I had the weirdest dream last night.\"

Sam told him everything. He explained the cabinet, the secret room, the flap, and most importantly, Paxman.

His dad listened carefully.

When Sam finished, his dad shook his head.

\"Sam, that's nonsense. It was just a dream.\"

\"But it felt real.\"

\"Dreams can feel real sometimes.\"

Sam sighed.

\"Okay...\"

He walked back to the kitchen.

If his dad didn't believe him, then he was on his own.

Sam thought for a moment.

Then he got an idea.

He prepared a plate full of bacon and placed it on the kitchen table.

\"If something really is taking bacon...\" Sam whispered, \"this should work.\"

He waited.

Then he looked at the plate.

He couldn't resist.

Sam grabbed a piece.

\"One won't hurt.\"

He thought for a second.

\"Maybe two.\"

He ate another piece and quickly put the plate back where it was.

Then he waited.

Nothing happened.

A few minutes passed.

Sam was beginning to think his dad was right.

Maybe it really had been a dream.

Then—

Creeeeak...

Sam's head snapped toward the cabinet.

The cabinet door slowly opened.

Sam's eyes widened.

He didn't move.

The door opened farther.

Sam knew it now.

What he had seen wasn't a dream.

It was a vision.

He quietly waited beside the kitchen table.

Something moved behind the cabinet.

Sam took a deep breath.

Then he slowly walked toward it.

He reached the cabinet.

His heart was pounding.

Sam grabbed the flap and pulled it open.

He stepped inside.

Everything was exactly as he remembered.

The hidden room.

The desk.

The papers.

The strange equipment.

And then—

He saw him.

Paxman.

He was sitting at the desk, doing research.

Sam froze.

It was exactly like his vision.

Paxman looked up.

\"Sam?\"

Sam stared at him in disbelief.

\"Paxman...\"

Paxman stood up.

For a moment, neither brother said anything.

Sam couldn't believe it.

His vision had come true.

Paxman was alive.

And now Sam needed to know why."}, {"n": 4, "title": "THE TRUTH", "body": "Sam stood in the secret room, staring at Paxman.

His mind was filled with questions.

\"Why?\" Sam finally asked. \"Why did you make a clone?\"

Paxman looked down.

\"I was under too much stress,\" he said. \"I couldn't handle everything that was happening. So I made a clone to do some of the things I couldn't.\"

Sam stared at him in disbelief.

\"You made a clone because you were stressed?\"

Paxman nodded.

\"And you sent the clone out there instead of telling me?\"

Sam's voice grew louder.

\"Well, you could've at least told your brother, Paxman!\"

Paxman looked hurt.

\"I thought you would make fun of me.\"

Sam's anger faded for a moment.

\"What?\"

\"I remember what happened at the orphanage,\" Paxman said. \"All the kids would make fun of me when I told them about how I figured out how to clone things.\"

He looked away.

\"They laughed at me. They thought I was weird.\"

Paxman looked back at Sam.

\"And I thought you might do the same thing.\"

Sam couldn't believe what he was hearing.

After everything they had been through together, Paxman still thought Sam would treat him like those kids did.

Sam shook his head.

Then, without saying another word, he turned and ran.

He rushed out of the secret room and down the hallway.

Tears filled his eyes.

\"I thought you knew me better than that!\" Sam cried.

He stopped for a moment and looked back.

\"After all those dreadful years in the wild, you still think I would do that?\"

Paxman stood in the doorway.

Tears were running down his face.

\"If I could do it again...\" Paxman said quietly, \"I would tell you.\"

Sam wiped his eyes.

\"I don't wanna hear about it.\"

Then he ran away.

Paxman remained standing in the doorway, watching his brother disappear.

For the first time since returning home, Paxman wondered if bringing himself back had only made everything worse."}, {"n": 5, "title": "THE AGENTS", "body": "Paxman knew things had to change.

He didn't want to keep secrets from Sam anymore.

So he started telling him everything.

He showed Sam his research, explained his ideas, and told him about all the things he had been working on.

At first, Sam was still upset.

But little by little, he began to understand.

Paxman wasn't trying to hurt him.

He was just scared.

Eventually, Sam finally forgave him.

\"Okay, Pax,\" Sam said. \"We're good.\"

Paxman smiled.

\"Thanks, Sam.\"

But before they could say anything else—

Paxman looked at one of the security cameras.

His smile disappeared.

\"Sam...\"

\"What?\"

\"Look outside.\"

Sam rushed over to the screen.

There were several people standing in their front yard.

They weren't neighbors.

They were agents.

\"Oh no,\" Paxman said.

\"What do we do?\" Sam whispered.

\"Hide!\"

The brothers quickly climbed into the secret room and pulled the cabinet shut behind them.

A few seconds later—

BANG!

The front door was kicked open.

Sam jumped.

The agents stormed into the house.

\"Search every room!\" one of them ordered.

Sam and Paxman stayed completely silent.

Inside the secret room, Paxman pulled up the security cameras.

They watched the agents search the house.

One agent walked through the kitchen.

Sam held his breath.

The agent stopped.

He looked around.

Then he walked away.

Paxman switched between the cameras as the agents continued searching.

\"They're getting closer,\" Sam whispered.

\"Stay quiet,\" Paxman replied.

The agents searched upstairs.

Then downstairs.

They checked the living room, the kitchen, and every bedroom.

Finally, one of the agents walked back toward the front door.

\"There's nobody here,\" he said.

Another agent nodded.

\"Let's search the next house.\"

The front door closed.

The house became quiet again.

Sam and Paxman waited.

Neither brother moved.

Finally, Paxman slowly opened the cabinet.

Sam peeked outside.

The agents were gone.

But Paxman knew one thing for sure.

They weren't safe.

Not anymore."}, {"n": 6, "title": "THE CALL", "body": "Paxman and Sam sat in the secret room, trying to figure out what to do next.

\"We have to leave,\" Paxman said.

Sam shook his head.

\"No. I'm staying.\"

\"Sam, the agents know we're here.\"

\"I don't care. I'm not leaving Dad.\"

Paxman sighed.

\"I didn't want to tell you this, but I think Dad keeps ratting us out.\"

Sam stared at him.

\"Oh, there you are again,\" Sam said angrily. \"Not telling me what's happening.\"

\"Sam, I'm just trying to keep us safe.\"

\"You know what? I'm staying. You can leave, but I'm not. I will stay. Dad is not going to tell those agents where we are. If you think he is, maybe you should leave.\"

Paxman looked at him.

\"Well... maybe I will.\"

The room became silent.

Neither brother said another word.

The next morning, Paxman started packing.

Sam stood nearby, watching him put his things into a bag.

\"So you're really leaving?\" Sam asked.

Paxman didn't answer.

He continued packing.

Sam looked away.

Then he heard something.

A quiet voice came from down the hallway.

Sam stopped.

It was his dad.

He was on the phone.

Sam moved closer to listen.

His dad spoke quietly.

\"Yes... they're here.\"

Sam's eyes widened.

He froze.

Paxman was right.

Their dad had told someone where they were.

Sam slowly turned toward Paxman.

\"We have a problem.\""}, {"n": 7, "title": "THE PRISON", "body": "Sam and Paxman didn't have much time.

They grabbed their things and left the house as quickly as they could.

But the moment they stepped outside, everything went wrong.

A police car suddenly came speeding around the corner.

\"RUN!\" Paxman yelled.

Sam took off.

He ran down one street, turned another corner, and then changed directions again.

\"Where do I go?\" Sam shouted.

The police car followed him.

Sam ran every which way, trying to lose them.

But eventually, he was caught.

The officers grabbed Sam and put him inside the police car.

Paxman saw what happened.

\"Sam!\"

Paxman took off running after the car.

He took a deep breath and used his heat breath.

A blast of heat shot toward the police car.

The car slowed down.

Sam looked around from inside.

He knew he couldn't just sit there.

He remembered his super strength.

\"Okay... let's do this.\"

Sam clenched his fist and punched the car door with all his strength.

BANG!

The door flew open.

Sam hopped out.

\"Go, Pax!\"

Paxman jumped into action, but he tripped on a bump.

\"Pax!\"

Sam ran over and tried to help him up.

Then suddenly—

A masked cop appeared behind them.

Before either brother could react, the officer grabbed them and snapped strange power-zapping handcuffs onto their wrists.

ZAP!

Sam jumped.

\"What was that?!\"

\"Power handcuffs,\" the masked cop said.

Paxman looked at the strange handcuffs.

\"We didn't do anything!\"

The cop didn't answer.

The brothers were put back into the police car.

This time, they couldn't escape.

The car drove for what seemed like hours.

Neither brother knew where they were going.

Finally, the car stopped.

Sam looked out the window.

His eyes widened.

\"No way...\"

Paxman looked out too.

A massive, dark prison stood in front of them.

Paxman stared at it.

\"Is that...?\"

\"Yeah,\" Sam whispered.

Azkaban.

The prison everyone thought only existed in the Harry Potter books.

Except this place was real.

And Azkaban was already scary enough without soul-sucking demons as guards.

The brothers were taken out of the car and led inside.

The enormous doors opened.

CREEEEAK...

Sam looked around.

The halls were dark and silent.

They passed rows of prison cells.

Some of the prisoners had been sent there for terrible crimes, including torture and murder.

Sam looked at Paxman.

\"This place is insane.\"

Paxman nodded.

\"I know.\"

A guard led them farther down the hallway.

Sam looked back toward the entrance.

The massive prison doors slowly closed behind them.

BOOM!

Sam swallowed.

\"We're trapped.\"

Paxman looked at his brother.

\"Not for long.\"

Sam raised an eyebrow.

\"What are you planning?\"

Paxman looked around the prison.

\"I'm still thinking.\"

The doors behind them locked.

CLICK.

The brothers looked at each other.

They were officially prisoners of Azkaban."}, {"n": 8, "title": "THE ESCAPE", "body": "Paxman and Sam stood in the dark hallway of Azkaban.

Neither brother knew what to do.

Then Paxman spotted something on the ground.

He bent down and picked it up.

It was a wand.

Paxman looked at Sam.

\"You thinking what I'm thinking?\"

Sam looked at the wand.

\"Let's use this to get out of here.\"

Paxman grinned.

He pointed the wand toward the prison bars.

\"Bombarda Maxima!\"

BOOM!

The bars burst open.

Sam jumped back.

\"Whoa!\"

Paxman looked at the opening.

\"Okay, let's run!\"

The brothers took off down the hallway."}], "available": true}];
let currentBook=null,currentChapter=0,fontSize=Number(localStorage.getItem('tgs-font')||20);
function esc(s){return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,m=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[m]))}
function renderLibrary(){const grid=document.getElementById('bookGrid');grid.innerHTML=books.map(b=>`<article class="book"><div class="cover b${b.id}"><div><div class="coverTitle">TWO GUYS STRANDED</div><div class="coverBook">${esc(b.tag)}</div></div><div><div class="coverSub">${esc(b.title)}</div><div class="coverAuthor">PAXMAN GADD</div></div></div><h3>${esc(b.tag)} — ${esc(b.title)}</h3><p>${b.id===6?'The Clone':b.id===5?'The Final Odyssey':'Adventure chapter book'}</p><button class="read" onclick="openBook(${b.id})">Read</button></article>`).join('')}
function parseLegacy(b){return b.chapters.map((t,i)=>({n:i+1,title:t,body:''}))}
function openBook(id){currentBook=books.find(b=>b.id===id);currentChapter=Number(localStorage.getItem('tgs-last-'+id)||0);document.getElementById('app').style.display='none';document.getElementById('reader').style.display='block';document.body.scrollTop=0;buildReader()}
function buildReader(){const ch=currentBook.chapters||[];document.getElementById('readerBook').textContent=currentBook.tag+' — '+currentBook.title;const sel=document.getElementById('chapterSelect');sel.innerHTML=ch.map((c,i)=>`<option value="${i}">Chapter ${c.n||i+1} — ${esc(c.title)}</option>`).join('');renderChapter()}
function renderChapter(){const ch=currentBook.chapters||[];if(!ch.length)return;currentChapter=Math.max(0,Math.min(currentChapter,ch.length-1));const c=ch[currentChapter];document.getElementById('chapterSelect').value=String(currentChapter);document.getElementById('chapterHeading').textContent='Chapter '+(c.n||currentChapter+1);document.getElementById('chapterSub').textContent=c.title;const text=document.getElementById('chapterText');text.textContent=c.body||'This chapter is listed from the manuscript supplied in chat. Full text is not embedded in this file yet.';text.style.fontSize=fontSize+'px';document.getElementById('prevBtn').disabled=currentChapter===0;document.getElementById('nextBtn').disabled=currentChapter===ch.length-1;document.getElementById('progress').style.width=((currentChapter+1)/ch.length*100)+'%';document.getElementById('status').textContent='Chapter '+(currentChapter+1)+' of '+ch.length;localStorage.setItem('tgs-last-'+currentBook.id,currentChapter)}
function goToChapter(i){currentChapter=i;renderChapter();window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}function nextChapter(){if(currentChapter<(currentBook.chapters.length-1)){currentChapter++;renderChapter();window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}}function previousChapter(){if(currentChapter>0){currentChapter--;renderChapter();window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}}function changeFont(n){fontSize=Math.max(15,Math.min(30,fontSize+n));localStorage.setItem('tgs-font',fontSize);renderChapter()}function closeReader(){document.getElementById('reader').style.display='none';document.getElementById('app').style.display='block';window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}
renderLibrary();
</script>
